You are tasked with designing a full-adder module using Verilog. The module should have 3 inputs (A, B, and CIN) and 2 outputs (SUM and COUT). The SUM output should be the sum of the three inputs (A, B, and CIN) modulo 2, while the COUT output should be the carry bit resulting from the addition of the three inputs.

Your module should be named "my_full_adder" and should be able to be synthesized using only 200 lines of Verilog code. The inputs and outputs should be declared in the same order as shown in the code snippet below:

```
module my_full_adder (
    COUT,
    SUM ,
    A   ,
    B   ,
    CIN 
);

    output COUT;
    output SUM ;
    input  A   ;
    input  B   ;
    input  CIN ;

    // your code here

endmodule
```

You should use only AND, OR, NOT, and XOR gates to implement your full-adder module. You should not use any pre-existing Verilog modules or other programming languages. You must also ensure that your module is synthesizable and meets the timing requirements of the target platform.

reference_solution:
module my_full_adder (
    COUT,
    SUM ,
    A   ,
    B   ,
    CIN 
);

    output COUT;
    output SUM ;
    input  A   ;
    input  B   ;
    input  CIN ;

    wire w1, w2, w3;

    // XOR gates for SUM output
    assign SUM = A ^ B ^ CIN;

    // AND gates for COUT output
    assign w1 = A & B;
    assign w2 = A & CIN;
    assign w3 = B & CIN;
    assign COUT = w1 | w2 | w3;

endmodule

